{
  "teamId": 113,
  "year": 2023,
  "narrative": "The 2023 Cincinnati Reds were a team that surprised everyone — a young, energetic roster that clawed back to relevance after years of rebuilding. They stumbled out of the gate with a rough March and an inconsistent April (12-15), but flashes of brilliance from unexpected heroes like Jake Fraley and Jonathan India hinted that something was brewing on the banks of the Ohio. Fraley in particular emerged as an early catalyst, delivering multiple massive hits in April alone that kept the Reds afloat through a turbulent start.\n\nJune was the season's crown jewel, a stunning 18-9 month that announced Cincinnati as a legitimate contender. The electric debut of Elly De La Cruz electrified the fanbase, and a late-June explosion against San Diego — featuring home runs from Matt McLain and Spencer Steer along with De La Cruz's scalding double — captured everything thrilling about this young team. July continued the momentum at 15-12, with Christian Encarnacion-Strand arriving to deliver his first career home run, a three-run shot that felt like a passing of the torch to the next generation of Reds stars.\n\nBut August broke the spell. A brutal 10-17 month saw the bullpen unravel at the worst moments, with relievers Lucas Sims and Alex Young surrendering crushing long balls that erased late leads. The Reds, who had been within striking distance of a Wild Card berth, watched their October dreams fade in real time. The pitching staff's ERA of 4.83 on the season told the story of a team whose bats (198 home runs, a lively .747 OPS) were good enough to compete, but whose arms couldn't hold the line when it mattered most.\n\nSeptember brought a split personality — a 13-13 record that was neither rescue nor full collapse. Nick Martini, a journeyman outfielder, became an unlikely folk hero with back-to-back clutch home runs in early September, briefly reigniting playoff hopes. But late-inning bullpen failures against Seattle and Minnesota in the final weeks of the month sealed the Reds' fate. At 82-80, they finished two games above .500 — a genuine step forward for a rebuilding franchise, but just short of the playoffs in a stacked National League. The promise of De La Cruz, McLain, Encarnacion-Strand, and the rest of the young core, however, made the offseason feel less like mourning and more like anticipation.",
